== Brauer Characters <sec:brauer-characters>

In this section, we interpret the decomposition map (defined in
§@ss:reduction-decomposition-map) in terms of characters.

=== Splitting Fields <ss:modular-splitting-fields>

Let $G$ be a finite group of exponent $m$; by definition, $m$ is the L.C.M. of
the orders of the elements of $G$. Following Serre~@bib:Serre1977, we say that a
field $E$ is _sufficiently large_#idx("sufficiently large field") (relative to
$G$) if $E$ contains all the $m$-th roots of unity. Let
#source(440)
$(K,R,k)$ be a $p$-modular system. In this subsection, we shall prove that if
$K$ is sufficiently large relative to $G$, then so is $k$, and of more
importance, both $K$ and $k$ are splitting fields for $G$ and all of its
subgroups.

If $char E=0$, then $E$ is sufficiently large relative to $G$ if and only if $E$
contains the cyclotomic field of $m$-th roots of unity. On the other hand, if
$char E=p>0$, write $m=p^a m'$, where $p divides.not m'$ (so $m'$ is the
$p'$-part of $m$). Then in $E[X]$ we have
$ X^m-1=(X^(m')-1)^(p^a), $
and thus $E$ contains the $m$-th roots of~1 if and only if $E$ contains the
$m'$-th roots of~1. The polynomial $X^(m')-1$ is separable over $E$, and its
roots form a cyclic group $⟨omega⟩$ of order $m'$, generated by a primitive
$m'$-th root of unity $omega$.

#theorem[
  If the field $E$ is sufficiently large relative to the finite group $G$, then
  $E$ is a splitting field for $G$ and all its subgroups.
] <th:sufficiently-large-field-splits-group>
#proof[
  If $E$ is sufficiently large for $G$, then clearly it is also sufficiently
  large for each subgroup of $G$, so it suffices to prove the theorem for $G$
  itself. If $char E=0$, the result follows from
  Corollary~@cor:exponent-cyclotomic-splitting-field. For the rest of the proof,
  we need only consider the case where $char E=p>0$. Let us write $m=p^a m'$ as
  above, where $m$ is the exponent of $G$ and $m'$ is its $p'$-part. Then by
  hypothesis, $E$ contains a primitive $m'$-th root of unity $omega$. Set
  $E_0=FF_p$, and let $k=E_0(omega) subset.eq E$. Then $k$ is a finite field,
  and by remark~(iv) following Definition~@def:splitting-field, it is sufficient
  to prove that $k$ is a splitting field for $G$.

  By Proposition~@prop:finite-splitting-field, there exists a finite extension
  $F$ of $k$ which is a splitting field for $G$. Then $F$ is a finite field, and
  is thus a Galois extension of $k$. Let $frak(G)$ denote the Galois group of
  $F$ relative to $k$. Suppose now that $U$ is a simple $k G$-module; we have to
  prove that $U$ is absolutely simple, and for this, it suffices (by
  @th:absolute-simplicity-centralizer) to show that $End_(k G) U=k dot 1_U$. Let
  $U^F=F times.o_k U$; then by
  Proposition~@prop:galois-orbit-simple-module-extension, $U^F$ is a direct sum
  of algebraic conjugates of a simple $F G$-module $M$, all occurring with the
  same multiplicity. By Corollary
  @cor:group-algebra-multiplicity-free-extension, this common multiplicity is
  equal to~1. If $M$ affords the $F$-character $mu$, then for each
  $sigma in frak(G)$, the algebraic conjugate module $attach(M, tl: sigma)$
  affords the character $attach(mu, tl: sigma)$. Moreover,
  $M tilde.equiv attach(M, tl: sigma)$ if and only if
  $attach(mu, tl: sigma)=mu$, by the Frobenius-Schur
  Theorem~@th:frobenius-schur-matrix-coordinates, which may be applied since $F$
  is a splitting field for $G$. On the other hand, all of the character values
  ${mu(x):x in G}$ lie in $k$, since $k$ contains all $m$-th roots of~1. Hence
  $attach(mu, tl: sigma)=mu$ for all $sigma in frak(G)$. Then
  $attach(M, tl: sigma) tilde.equiv M$ for all $sigma$, and since the
  multiplicity of $M$ as a summand of $U^F$ is~1, we have $U^F tilde.equiv M$.
  Then $End_(F G) M=F dot 1_M$, and it follows that $End_(k G) U=k dot 1_U$,
  completing the proof.
]

#corollary[
  Let $(K,R,k)$ be a $p$-modular system and assume $char K=0$. If $K$ is
  sufficiently large relative to the finite group $G$, then $k$ is also
  sufficiently large, and both $K$ and $k$ are splitting fields for $G$ and all
  of its subgroups.
] <cor:modular-system-sufficiently-large-fields>
#source(441)
#proof[
  Let $m$ be the exponent of $G$, $m=p^a m'$, where $p divides.not m'$, and let
  $omega$ be a primitive $m$-th root of~1 in $K$. Then $omega in R$, since the
  equation $omega^m-1=0$ shows that $omega in "alg. int."{K} subset.eq R$. We
  have
  $ X^m-1=product_(i=0)^(m-1) (X-omega^i) quad "in" R[X]. $
  If bars denote passage to the residue class field $k$ $(=R slash frak(p))$, we
  have
  $
    (X^(m')-overline(1))^(p^a)=X^m-overline(1)
    =product_(i=0)^(m-1) (X-overline(omega^i)) quad "in" k[X].
  $
  Therefore the powers ${overline(omega^i):0<=i<=m-1}$ give all the $m'$-th
  roots of~1, each with multiplicity $p^a$. Each $overline(omega^i)$ lies in
  $k$, since $omega^i in R$, so $k$ contains all the $m'$-th (and $m$-th) roots
  of~1. The result now follows from @th:sufficiently-large-field-splits-group.
]

The ideas used in establishing Theorem~@th:sufficiently-large-field-splits-group
can also be used to prove:

#theorem[
  For $E$ an arbitrary field, and $G$ a finite group, the characters afforded by
  a basic set of simple $E G$-modules are linearly independent over $E$.
] <th:modular-irreducible-character-independence>
#proof[
  Let ${M_1,dots,M_d}$ be a basic set of simple $E G$-modules affording the
  characters ${mu_1,dots,mu_d}$, respectively. For the case where $char E=0$,
  see @prop:irreducible-group-characters-independent. Another proof may be found
  in @bib:CurtisReiner1962[CR (30.12)].

  Now let $char E=p>0$. From §@sec:separable-algebras-splitting-fields, there
  exists an extension field $F$ of $E$ which is a splitting field for $G$. Let
  ${W_1,dots,W_e}$ be a basic set of simple $F G$-modules affording the
  characters ${omega_1,dots,omega_e}$, respectively. By
  @cor:group-algebra-multiplicity-free-extension, each module $M_i^F$ is a sum
  of simple modules ${W_j}$, no two of which are isomorphic. By
  Exercise~@exc:hom-field-extension, no simple module appears as a common
  summand of $M_i^F$ and $M_j^F$ if $i!=j$. The characters
  ${omega_1,dots,omega_e}$ are linearly independent over $F$ by the
  Frobenius-Schur Theorem~@th:frobenius-schur-matrix-coordinates. We leave to
  the reader the easy exercise of combining these remarks to deduce that
  ${mu_1,dots,mu_d}$ are linearly independent over $E$, and the proof is
  complete.
]

=== Brauer Characters <ss:modular-brauer-characters>

We come now to some fundamental ideas due to Brauer. Let $(K,R,k)$ be a
$p$-modular system such that $char K=0$, and let $G$ be a finite group. By
@th:modular-irreducible-character-independence, the characters of a basic set of
simple $k G$-modules are linearly independent. The simplest examples show,
however, that two $k G$-modules can have the same $k$-character without being
isomorphic or even having the same set of composition factors. (For example,
take the direct sum of $p$ copies and of $2p$ copies of the trivial
representation of $G$, where $p=char k$; the characters of both representations
are zero for all elements of $G$.) The usefulness of
#source(442)
characters in fields of characteristic zero, for investigations of finite
groups, provides strong motivation for seeking a satisfactory way of handling
characters of $k G$-modules without losing too much information. Brauer found an
ingenious way out of this dilemma by associating with each $k G$-module $L$ a
$K$-valued function $lambda$, defined on the set $G_(p')$ of $p$-regular
elements of $G$. As we shall see, these functions yield most of the desired
information.

Throughout this subsection, we fix the following notation:

$(K,R,k)$, a $p$-modular system such that $char K=0$, with $K$ sufficiently
large relative to a given finite group $G$;

$m="exponent of" G$, $m=p^a m'$, $p divides.not m'$;

$omega="primitive" m' "-th root of 1 in" K$;

$f:R->k$ $(=R slash frak(p))$ the natural homomorphism, also denoted by
$xi mapsto overline(xi)$, $xi in R$.

By Corollary~@cor:modular-system-sufficiently-large-fields, $k$ is sufficiently
large relative to $G$, and both $K$ and $k$ are splitting fields for $G$ and all
of its subgroups. By the proof of
Corollary~@cor:modular-system-sufficiently-large-fields, $overline(omega)$ is a
primitive $m'$-th root of~1 in $k$, and $f:⟨omega⟩->⟨overline(omega)⟩$ is an
isomorphism from the cyclic group of $m'$-th roots of~1 in $K$ onto the
corresponding cyclic group in $k$.

Let $L$ be a left $k G$-module (always assumed f.g.). For each $p$-regular
element $x in G_(p')$, all the eigenvalues ${xi_1,dots,xi_d}$ of $x_l$ on $L$
are $m'$-th roots of~1, and hence can be expressed as powers of
$overline(omega)$, say ${overline(omega)^(i_1),dots,overline(omega)^(i_d)}$,
where $d=dim_k L$. Then define
$ lambda(x)=omega^(i_1)+dots+omega^(i_d), $
or, in terms of the eigenvalues ${xi_i}$ of $x_l$,
$ lambda(x)=sum_(i=1)^d f^(-1)lr((xi_i)). $
It is clear from the definition that $lambda:G_(p')->K$ is a well-defined
$K$-valued function on $G_(p')$. Note also that once the $p$-modular system
$(K,R,k)$ is fixed, there is no ambiguity in the definition of $lambda$; the
isomorphism $f:⟨omega⟩ tilde.equiv ⟨overline(omega)⟩$ arises from the canonical
map $R->R slash frak(p)=k$.

#definition[
  For each left $k G$-module $L$, the $K$-valued function $lambda:G_(p')->K$
  defined above is called the _Brauer character_#footnote[
    Brauer called $lambda$ a “modular character” of $G$.
  ] of $G$ _afforded by_ $L$. The trace function
  $ x mapsto op("Tr")(x,L),x in G, $
  is called the $k$-character of $L$.
] <def:brauer-character>
#idx("Brauer characters", target: [@def:brauer-character])
#idx("character", target: [@def:brauer-character])
#idx("character", "afforded by a module", target: [@def:brauer-character])
#idx("character", "Brauer", target: [@def:brauer-character])

#source(443)
We now collect some elementary facts about Brauer characters.

#proposition[
  #enum(
    numbering: "(i)",
    [The Brauer character $lambda$ afforded by a left $k G$-module $L$ is a
      class function on the $p$-regular classes of $G$.],
    [A Brauer character $lambda$ afforded by a $k G$-module $L$ takes values in
      $R$, and we have
      $ overline(lambda(x))=op("Tr")(x,L),x in G_(p'). $],
    [Let $L_0 supset L_1 supset 0$ be $k G$-modules. Let $chi$ be the Brauer
      character afforded by $L_0 slash L_1$, $chi_1$ the Brauer character
      afforded by $L_1$ and $chi_0$ the Brauer character of $L_0$. Then
      $chi_0=chi+chi_1$.],
    [Let $V$ be a $K G$-module with $K$-character $chi$. Then for each full
      $R G$-lattice $M$ in $V$, the restriction $chi|_(G_(p'))$ of $chi$ to
      $G_(p')$ is the Brauer character of the $k G$-module $overline(M)$
      $(=M slash frak(p)M)$.],
  )
] <prop:brauer-character-properties>
#proof[
  (i) and (iii) are left as exercises for the reader. (ii) is immediate, since
  $lambda(x)$ is a sum of roots of~1, hence in $R$, and $overline(lambda(x))$ is
  the sum of the eigenvalues of the left multiplication $x_l$ on $L$, so
  $overline(lambda(x))=op("Tr")(x,L)$.

  (iv) Let $upright(bold(M))$ be the matrix representation of $G$ afforded by
  $V$, with respect to an $R$-basis of the full $R G$-lattice $M$ in $V$. For
  $x in G_(p')$, the eigenvalues ${xi_i}_(1<=i<=d)$ of $upright(bold(M))(x)$
  belong to $R$, and
  $ chi(x)=xi_1+dots+xi_d. $
  The result will follow if we can show that
  ${overline(xi_1),dots,overline(xi_d)}$ are the eigenvalues of
  $overline(upright(bold(M)))(x)$, since $overline(upright(bold(M)))$ is a
  matrix representation afforded by the $k G$-module $overline(M)$. The matrix
  $upright(bold(M))(x)$ has entries in $R$, so
  $"char. pol." upright(bold(M))(x) in R[X]$, and
  $ "char. pol." upright(bold(M))(x)=product_(i=1)^d (X-xi_i) quad "in" R[X]. $
  The polynomial obtained from $"char. pol." upright(bold(M))(x)$ by reducing
  coefficients mod $frak(p)$, is $"char. pol." overline(upright(bold(M)))(x)$.
  Therefore
  $
    "char. pol." overline(upright(bold(M)))(x)
    =product_(i=1)^d (X-overline(xi_i)) quad "in" k[X].
  $
  It follows that ${overline(xi_i)}_(1<=i<=d)$ are the eigenvalues of
  $overline(upright(bold(M)))(x)$, and (iv) is proved.
]

