Letting $zeta^i$ be the $K$-character afforded by $Z_i$ as in
@def:character-table-data, for $1<=i<=s$, the decomposition map (see
§@ss:reduction-decomposition-map) is given in terms of Brauer characters by
$ zeta^i|_(G_(p'))=sum_(j=1)^r d_(i j) phi^j quad "on" G_(p'), $
<eq:decomposition-brauer-character-values>
where $upright(bold(D))=(d_(i j))$ is the decomposition matrix
(@def:decomposition-map-matrix). The proof of
@eq:decomposition-brauer-character-values is an application of
Proposition~@prop:brauer-character-properties, and is left as an exercise for
the reader.

Our next objective is to prove, following Serre~@bib:Serre1977, that the
irreducible Brauer characters ${phi^1,dots,phi^r}$ are linearly independent over
$K$. Since $k$ is sufficiently large, we already know by the Frobenius-Schur
Theorem that the $k$-characters afforded by the modules ${F_1,dots,F_r}$ are
linearly independent over $k$.

#lemma[
  Let $upright(bold(M)):G->GL_n lr((k))$ be a matrix representation of $G$ over
  $k$. Let $x in G$, and let $x=s u$ be its decomposition into a $p$-regular
  part $s$ and a $p$-part $u$. Then the matrices $upright(bold(M))(x)$ and
  $upright(bold(M))(s)$ have the same set of eigenvalues, counted with
  multiplicities.
] <lem:modular-character-regular-part-eigenvalues>
#proof[
  We have $upright(bold(M))(x)=upright(bold(M))(s)upright(bold(M))(u)$, and the
  matrices $upright(bold(M))(s)$ and $upright(bold(M))(u)$ commute with each
  other. Therefore the eigenvalues of $upright(bold(M))(x)$ are the pairwise
  products of suitably ordered sets of eigenvalues of $upright(bold(M))(s)$ and
  $upright(bold(M))(u)$. Since $u$ is a $p$-element, and $k$ has characteristic
  $p$, it follows that a $p$-power of $upright(bold(M))(u)$ is equal to the
  identity, and therefore all the eigenvalues of $upright(bold(M))(u)$ are equal
  to~1. Therefore the eigenvalues of $upright(bold(M))(x)$ coincide with those
  of $upright(bold(M))(s)$, as required.
]

#theorem[
  The irreducible Brauer characters ${phi^1,dots,phi^r}$ form a $K$-basis of the
  space of $K$-valued class functions $cf_K lr((G_(p')))$.
] <th:brauer-characters-class-function-basis>
#proof[
  We first prove that ${phi^1,dots,phi^r}$ are linearly independent over $K$.
  Suppose not, and consider a nontrivial relation of linear dependence with
  #source(445)
  coefficients in $K$. Since $K$ is the quotient field of $R$, we can clear
  denominators, and obtain such a relation with coefficients in $R$. Every
  nonunit in $R$ is divisible by a power of $pi$, so we can divide by some
  appropriate power of $pi$, to arrive at a relation
  $ sum_(i=1)^r a_i phi^i lr((s))=0 quad forall s in G_(p'), $
  where the $a_i in R$, and some $a_(i_0) in.not frak(p)$. Reducing mod
  $frak(p)$, we obtain
  $
    sum_(i=1)^r overline(a_i) overline(phi^i lr((s)))=0 quad forall s in G_(p'),
  $
  with at least one coefficient $overline(a_(i_0))!=0$ in $k$. By
  @prop:brauer-character-properties(ii), we can rewrite this relation as
  $ sum_(i=1)^r overline(a_i) op("Tr")(s,F_i)=0 quad forall s in G_(p'). $
  But for $x in G$, $op("Tr")(x,F_i)$ is the sum of the eigenvalues of $x_l$ on
  $F_i$; it follows from @lem:modular-character-regular-part-eigenvalues that
  $ sum_(i=1)^r overline(a_i) op("Tr")(x,F_i)=0, forall x in G. $
  Since $overline(a_(i_0))!=0$, we have obtained a nontrivial relation of linear
  dependence among the $k$-characters of the simple $k G$-modules ${F_i}$,
  contrary to the Frobenius-Schur Theorem (see also
  @th:modular-irreducible-character-independence). Thus ${phi^1,dots,phi^r}$ are
  linearly independent over $K$.

  Now let $xi in cf_K lr((G_(p')))$ be an arbitrary $K$-valued class function on
  $G_(p')$. Then $xi$ can be extended to a class function $xi^("#")$ on $G$, for
  example by setting $xi^("#")(x)=0$ for $x in.not G_(p')$. From
  §@ss:finite-group-character-orthogonality, this extension $xi^("#")$ is a
  $K$-linear combination of the characters ${zeta^1,dots,zeta^s}$ in
  $op("Irr")_K G$. Then
  $ xi^("#")(x)=sum_j alpha_j zeta^j lr((x)) quad forall x in G, $
  for some coefficients ${alpha_j}$ in $K$. Restricting to $x in G_(p')$, we
  have
  $ xi=sum_j alpha_j zeta^j|_(G_(p')). $
  By @eq:decomposition-brauer-character-values, $zeta^j|_(G_(p'))$ is a
  $K$-linear combination of the irreducible Brauer characters
  ${phi^1,dots,phi^r}$, and hence the same is true for $xi$. This completes the
  proof of the theorem.
]

#source(446)
There are a number of important corollaries of the preceding theorem. The first
answers the question of how two $k G$-modules with the same Brauer character are
related. They are not necessarily isomorphic, but at least they determine the
same element of the Grothendieck group $G_0(k G)$.

#corollary[
  Let $L$ and $M$ be $k G$-modules with Brauer characters $lambda$ and $mu$,
  respectively. If $lambda=mu$, then $L$ and $M$ have the same set of
  composition factors, and hence $[L]=[M]$ in $G_0(k G)$.
] <cor:brauer-character-composition-factors>
#proof[
  Suppose $L$ and $M$ have composition series such that the simple module $F_j$
  appears $a_j$ times in $L$ and $b_j$ times in $M$. By
  @prop:brauer-character-properties(iii), it follows that
  $ lambda=sum_(j=1)^r a_j phi^j,mu=sum_(j=1)^r b_j phi^j. $
  Then, by Theorem~@th:brauer-characters-class-function-basis, $lambda=mu$
  implies $a_j=b_j$, $1<=j<=r$, and the result follows.
]

The next corollary settles the important problem of determining the number of
simple $k G$-modules in a basic set. Another proof of this fundamental result
(due to Brauer), which does not involve Brauer characters, and is of independent
interest, can be found in @bib:CurtisReiner1962[CR §83B].

#corollary[
  The number of absolutely simple $E G$-modules, for a splitting field $E$ of
  characteristic $p$, is equal to the number of $p$-regular conjugacy classes of
  $G$.
] <cor:brauer-number-simple-modules>
#proof[
  We first show that it is sufficient to prove the result for $k G$-modules,
  where $k$ is part of our $p$-modular system $(K,R,k)$. Indeed, there exists a
  composite field extension of $E$ and $k$. Hence by
  §@sec:separable-algebras-splitting-fields, the number of simple $E G$-modules
  coincides with the number of simple $k G$-modules. Thus we have to prove that
  the number of simple $k G$-modules is equal to the number of conjugacy classes
  of $G$ contained in $G_(p')$. But this is clear by
  Theorem~@th:brauer-characters-class-function-basis, since the Brauer
  characters ${phi^1,dots,phi^r}$ form a $K$-basis for the space of $K$-valued
  class functions on $G_(p')$.
]

We shall now interpret the decomposition map in terms of Brauer characters.

#definition[
  A _virtual Brauer character_#idx("virtual characters") #idx(
    "character",
    "virtual",
  ) on $G$ is a $ZZ$-linear combination of Brauer characters of $k G$-modules.
  The set of virtual Brauer characters of $G$ is denoted by $"Bch" k G$, or
  simply $"Bch" G$.
] <def:virtual-brauer-character>

#lemma[
  Let $L$ and $M$ be $k G$-modules affording the Brauer characters $lambda$ and
  $mu$, respectively. Then $lambda mu$ is the Brauer character of the
  $k G$-module $L times.o_k M$.
] <lem:brauer-character-tensor-product>
#source(447)
#proof[
  Let $x in G_(p')$. Then $x_l$ is a semisimple linear transformation on $L$ and
  $M$. By choosing bases of eigenvectors for $x_l$ in $L$ and $M$, and a basis
  of $L times.o_k M$ consisting of tensor products of these basis elements, it
  follows that the eigenvalues of $x_l$ on $L times.o_k M$ are the products of
  eigenvalues of $x_l$ on $L$ with the eigenvalues of $x_l$ on $M$. Since the
  Brauer character is defined in terms of eigenvalues, we obtain the result of
  the Lemma.
]

#proposition[
  The set of virtual Brauer characters $"Bch" k G$ is a ring under addition and
  multiplication of functions. The map, which assigns to an element
  $t in G_0(k G)$ the virtual Brauer character $tau$ associated with it, defines
  an isomorphism of $ZZ$-algebras:
  $ G_0(k G) tilde.equiv "Bch" k G. $
] <prop:grothendieck-brauer-character-ring>
#idx("character", "ring", target: [@prop:grothendieck-brauer-character-ring])
#idx(
  "virtual characters",
  "ring of",
  target: [@prop:grothendieck-brauer-character-ring],
)
#proof[
  Let us first explain what is meant by the virtual Brauer character associated
  with an element $t in G_0(k G)$. By @prop:artinian-grothendieck-simple-basis,
  ${[F_1],dots,[F_r]}$ is a $ZZ$-basis of $G_0(k G)$, so we can write
  $ t=sum_(i=1)^r a_i [F_i], $
  with uniquely determined coefficients ${a_i}$ in $ZZ$. The virtual Brauer
  character $tau$ corresponding to $t$ is then defined by
  $ tau=sum_(i=1)^r a_i phi^i. $
  We next observe that by Theorem~@th:brauer-characters-class-function-basis,
  ${phi^1,dots,phi^r}$ forms a $ZZ$-basis of $"Bch" k G$. Therefore the above
  map $t mapsto tau$ is an isomorphism of free $ZZ$-modules. By
  Lemma~@lem:brauer-character-tensor-product and
  @eq:grothendieck-tensor-multiplication, it follows that the map is an
  isomorphism of $ZZ$-algebras, completing the proof.
]

#proposition[
  There is a commutative diagram
  #decomposition-brauer-square
  where the horizontal maps are the isomorphisms defined in
  @prop:grothendieck-character-ring and
  @prop:grothendieck-brauer-character-ring. The vertical map $d$ is the
  decomposition map, and $d'$ is the restriction map $psi mapsto psi|_(G_(p'))$
  of a virtual character $psi in "ch" K G$ to $G_(p')$.
] <prop:decomposition-brauer-character-square>
#proof[
  By @eq:decomposition-brauer-character-values, the diagram commutes when we
  compute the image of each element $[Z_i]$ in $G_0(K G)$ corresponding to a
  simple $K G$-module $Z_i$. Since these elements form a $ZZ$-basis of
  $G_0(K G)$, the result follows.
]

#source(448)
We conclude this section with some additional remarks about
Proposition~@prop:normal-p-subgroup-radical-kernel:

#proposition[
  Let $D$ be a normal $p$-subgroup of $G$, and let $(K,R,k)$ be an arbitrary
  $p$-modular system. Then
  #enum(
    numbering: "(i)",
    [$D$ acts trivially on every simple $k G$-module $V$, and therefore the
      simple $k G$-modules coincide with the simple $k(G slash D)$-modules.],
    [The natural surjection $tau:R G->R(G slash D)$ has the property that
      $ker tau$ is nilpotent mod $frak(p) G$, and hence
      $ker tau subset.eq rad R G$.],
  )
] <prop:normal-p-subgroup-brauer-kernel>
#proof[
  If $I$ is the augmentation ideal of $k D$, then $I dot k G=k G dot I$, and
  thus $I dot V$ is a $k G$-submodule of $V$. Since $I dot V!=V$ because $I$ is
  nilpotent, we deduce that $I dot V=0$, and thus $D$ acts trivially on $V$.

  Alternatively, we may note that by Clifford's Theorem~@th:clifford-theorem,
  $V_D$ is a semisimple $k D$-module, and hence is $D$-trivial by
  @th:p-group-algebra-local.

  The remainder of the proof is as in @prop:normal-p-subgroup-radical-kernel.
]

#example[
  Let $G=SL_2(p)$ for an odd prime $p$. Let $(K,R,k)$ be a $p$-modular system
  with $char K=0$ and $K$ sufficiently large relative to $G$. By definition, $G$
  is the group of $2 times 2$ matrices of determinant~1 with entries in the
  prime field $FF_p$. Using linear algebra, the reader can show as an exercise
  that the conjugacy class of a $p$-regular element of $G$ is determined by its
  characteristic equation, and that there are exactly $p$ $p$-regular conjugacy
  classes. By @cor:brauer-number-simple-modules, there are exactly $p$ simple
  $k G$-modules. Here is their construction. For each $d$, $0<=d<=p-1$, let
  $M_d$ be the $k$-space of homogeneous polynomials of degree $d$ in two
  indeterminates $X$ and $Y$. The group $G$ acts on $k[X,Y]$ as a group of
  automorphisms, where for $g=mat(alpha, beta; gamma, delta) in G$, we let
  $ g X=alpha X+gamma Y,g Y=beta X+delta Y. $
  The subspaces ${M_d}_(0<=d<=p-1)$ of $k[X,Y]$ are $k G$-submodules of
  dimensions $1,2,dots,p$, respectively. We show that each $M_d$ is
  simple.#ed-note[
    The printed argument asserts that the monomial lines have pairwise distinct
    diagonal characters. For $p=3$ and $d=1$, these characters are $a$ and
    $a^(-1)=a$, so this assertion fails.
  ] Let $0!=W subset.eq M_d$ be a $k G$-submodule, and choose
  $ f=sum_(i=0)^j a_i X^(d-i)Y^i in W,a_j!=0. $
  For $t in FF_p$, the upper unipotent matrix $U(t)=mat(1, t; 0, 1)$ sends $X$
  to $X$ and $Y$ to $t X+Y$. The polynomial $U(t)f$ in $t$ has degree
  $j<=d<=p-1$, with leading coefficient $a_j X^d$. Evaluation at the $p$
  distinct elements of $FF_p$ gives an invertible Vandermonde matrix on
  polynomials of degree at most $p-1$. Each coefficient of $U(t)f$ is therefore
  a $k$-linear combination of its values, all of which lie in $W$. Hence
  $a_j X^d in W$, and $X^d in W$.

  The lower unipotent matrices $mat(1, 0; t, 1)$ send $X^d$ to $(X+t Y)^d$.
  Applying the same interpolation argument to this polynomial in $t$ shows that
  $ binom(d, i)X^(d-i)Y^i in W,0<=i<=d. $
  Since $d<p$, every $binom(d, i)$ is nonzero in $k$. Thus $W=M_d$, proving
  simplicity for every $0<=d<=p-1$. These $p$ simple modules are pairwise
  non-isomorphic because their dimensions $1,2,dots,p$ differ. Finally, by
  comparing $dim_k M_d$ with $|G|$, the reader can show that the dimensions of
  the simple $k G$-modules need not divide $|G|$.
] <exm:sl2-prime-modular-simple-modules>

