#exercises-heading

#exercise[
  Find the Brauer characters of the symmetric groups $S_3$ and $S_4$ for the
  $2$-modular system defined in Examples~(i) and~(ii) at the end of
  §@sec:decomposition-map. Compute the Brauer characters after a compatible
  extension to a sufficiently large $p$-modular system; their rational values
  then descend to the systems in those examples.
] <exc:symmetric-brauer-characters-two>

#exercise[
  Referring to Proposition~@prop:decomposition-brauer-character-square, show
  that the kernel of the decomposition map $d:G_0(K G)->G_0(k G)$ corresponds to
  the set of virtual characters
  $ {psi in "ch" K G:psi|_(G_(p'))=0}. $
] <exc:decomposition-kernel-character-restriction>

#exercise[
  Prove Corollary~@cor:modular-system-sufficiently-large-fields without the
  assumption that $char K=0$.
] <exc:modular-system-large-fields-positive-characteristic>

#exercise[
  Show that for any field $E$, the ring $G_0(E G)$ has no nonzero nilpotent
  elements.
  #hint([@exc:grothendieck-group-ring-reduced])[
    Choose a field $E' supset.eq E$ which is sufficiently large. The map
    $beta:G_0(E G)->G_0(E' G)$, given by $[V] mapsto [E' times.o_E V]$ for each
    $E G$-module $V$, is a ring homomorphism. If $char E=0$, then $E G$ is
    separable, and Exercise~@exc:separable-grothendieck-injection shows that
    $beta$ is monic. If $char E=p>0$, then
    Corollary~@cor:group-algebra-multiplicity-free-extension expresses the
    scalar extension of each simple $E G$-module as a nonempty sum of pairwise
    non-isomorphic simple $E' G$-modules. By Exercise~@exc:hom-field-extension,
    non-isomorphic simple $E G$-modules have extensions with disjoint simple
    supports. Since the classes of simple modules form a $ZZ$-basis of each
    Grothendieck group, this also proves that $beta$ is monic.

    It therefore suffices to show that $G_0(E' G)$ is reduced. In characteristic
    zero it is isomorphic to the ordinary-character ring $"ch" E' G$, a subring
    of the ring of $E'$-valued functions on $G$. In characteristic $p>0$, choose
    a $p$-modular system $(K,R,k)$ with $char K=0$ and $K$ sufficiently large.
    Then $k$ is also a splitting field by
    @cor:modular-system-sufficiently-large-fields. A common extension of the
    splitting fields $E'$ and $k$ induces ring isomorphisms on their
    Grothendieck groups, because scalar extension carries each basic set of
    absolutely simple modules bijectively to a basic set over the common field.
    Thus $G_0(E' G) tilde.equiv G_0(k G) tilde.equiv "Bch" k G$. The latter is a
    subring of the ring of $K$-valued functions on $G_(p')$, so it has no
    nonzero nilpotent elements.
  ]
] <exc:grothendieck-group-ring-reduced>

#exercise[
  Show that the number of $p$-regular conjugacy classes in a direct product
  $G times H$ equals the product of the corresponding numbers for $G$ and $H$.
  This result can be used (see §@ss:outer-tensor-products) to complete the proof
  that for $k$ sufficiently large, every simple $k(G times H)$-module can be
  expressed as an outer tensor product of simple $k G$- and $k H$-modules.
] <exc:brauer-class-number-direct-product>

#exercise[
  Determine the Brauer characters of the simple $k G$-modules, for a finite
  abelian group $G$, with respect to a $p$-modular system such that $p$ divides
  $|G|$.
] <exc:abelian-group-simple-brauer-characters>
